# vergeio_network_rule (Resource)

`vergeio_network_rule` is one firewall rule, matched by name on the network. Use it when rules are split across modules. `apply` defaults to true and refreshes the network after a change to this rule. Set `apply` to false on every rule except the last, and make that last rule `depends_on` the staged ones, so the network reloads once. When every rule sets `apply` to false, [`vergeio_network_apply`](../actions/network_apply.html) refreshes the running network.

Each refresh reloads the whole network. Prefer [`vergeio_network_rules`](network_rules.md) when one configuration owns every rule. Do not use both resources on the same network. `vergeio_network_rules` deletes non-system rules it does not list, including rules created here.

System rules cannot be changed or deleted. A stopped network is not refreshed; it loads staged rules when it starts. With `apply` set to false, Terraform leaves `need_fw_apply` set and warns. VergeOS may act on a stale pending flag on its own.

An omitted optional attribute, such as `source_ip` or `orderid`, is left as it is on the network. `direction`, `action`, `protocol`, `interface`, and the boolean flags have defaults and are always written.

VergeOS keeps a rule's position as `orderid` on the `vnet_rules` row and renumbers rules from 1 through the number of non-system rules. There is no separate move action. A configured `orderid` in that range is written on the row, including after create, and the rule ends up there. Several `vergeio_network_rule` resources on one network can set `orderid` in the same apply; each rule is moved into its position. A value outside that range, such as 10 when the network has 3 rules, fails the apply. The error names the range. Leave `orderid` unset to keep the position VergeOS assigns, or use [`vergeio_network_rules`](network_rules.md) when one configuration owns the list. If Terraform applies the rules one at a time, a higher `orderid` is rejected until that many rules exist, so make it depend on the earlier rules or leave `orderid` unset. Refresh reads the position VergeOS has. A configured `orderid` that no longer matches, because another rule moved, is a normal plan diff. An omitted `orderid` stays as stored when nothing else on the rule changes, and is left unknown for that apply when the rule itself changes, because other rules applied at the same time can renumber it. `pin` `top` or `bottom` also asks VergeOS to reorder. The stored position still has to match a configured `orderid`.

